---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Figure from '../components/Figure.astro';
import CardCount from '../components/CardCount.astro';
import EolCharts from '../islands/EolCharts.tsx';
import { cardCount, fig, formatCount } from '../lib/data';

// the English OpenList's valid list at the pinned revision, and the part of
// it version 1.0 ships; the rest is restricted or held back
const atPin = cardCount('model_basis_entries');
const shipped = fig('rows.eol').value;
const gap = atPin - shipped;
if (gap < 0) throw new Error('version 1.0 ships more of the English OpenList than its list held');
---

<Base
  title="English OpenList"
  section="English OpenList"
  description="The English OpenList, a word list with its own rules, as a dated subset of the CEOL Universe: what it is, how it decided, and its rows charted."
>
  <div class="frame eol">
    <header class="eol-head">
      <svg class="eol-mark" viewBox="0 0 48 48" width="56" height="56" aria-hidden="true">
        <rect x="2" y="2" width="44" height="44" fill="none" stroke="var(--rule-strong)" stroke-width="2"></rect>
        <rect x="9" y="23" width="16" height="16" fill="var(--eol)"></rect>
      </svg>
      <div>
        <h1>English OpenList</h1>
        <p class="lede">
          A word list with its own rules and its own record of where each entry came from. Inside the universe it is a dated
          subset, fixed at 1 revision of its list.
        </p>
      </div>
    </header>

    <section class="prose eol-facts" aria-label="The English OpenList in the universe">
      <p class="eol-sum">
        Its valid list held <CardCount name="model_basis_entries" /> entries at revision <code>c1139698</code>, and
        version 1.0 ships <strong class="is-eol"><Figure id="rows.eol" /></strong> of them. The license rules keep the
        other {formatCount(gap)} out of the public version.
      </p>
      <p>
        The universe reads the English OpenList at that 1 revision and never writes to it. A row's <code>eol</code> column says
        whether the list held it then: a fact about the list at a date, never a verdict of the universe's.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="prose eol-about" aria-labelledby="about-eol">
      <h2 id="about-eol">What it is</h2>
      <p>
        The English OpenList is a free English word list for word games and apps, for researchers and teachers, for
        developers who need a list they can drop into their code, and for anyone who wants to download one. It began in 2026
        because good English word lists are often paywalled, restrictively licensed, or stale copies of unknown origin.
        Projects built on it include <a href="https://ars-magna.pages.dev/">Ars Magna</a>,
        <a href="https://amordle.vercel.app/">Amordle</a>, and
        <a href="https://ryanjosephkamp.github.io/portfolios/anagram-tiles/">Anagram Tiles</a>.
      </p>
      <p>
        Its entries came in by several routes: a tournament word list, a verification pipeline that used a language model,
        its synthetic engine, and a daily search for new words. Its later additions were checked against Merriam-Webster;
        the entries from its earlier intakes were not all checked. The charts below show how its entries came in.
      </p>
      <p>
        It is deliberately not Scrabble-legal. At the pinned revision it holds <CardCount name="eol_one_letter" /> one-letter
        entries and <CardCount name="eol_over_45" /> entries longer than 45 characters, the longest
        <CardCount name="eol_longest" /> characters long; tournament lists exclude both. Keep lengths 2 to 45 for a
        Scrabble-shaped subset.
      </p>
      <p>
        It published a new list each day from January to September 24, 2026, the date of its last published list. In this
        dataset it is a dated subset: version 1.0 holds its list at 1 revision, and a later version takes in what it adds
        after that by moving the pin. The config keeps the English
        OpenList's own name for its list, <code>eol-valid</code>; this site never calls an entry valid.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="prose eol-how" aria-labelledby="how">
      <h2 id="how">How it decided its list</h2>
      <p>
        The English OpenList took strings in from many channels and ruled on each: valid or invalid. Its rules included a
        strong-attestation rule, a string that 8 of its 12 sources list; a verification pipeline that used a language model,
        Google Gemini 3 Flash Preview; and a synthetic engine.
      </p>
      <p>
        The synthetic engine made <CardCount name="generated_rows" /> of the universe's rows. Its code does not survive, and
        its own per-row record of the method is empty, but its author describes the methods: affixes applied without a
        part-of-speech filter to the list as it then stood (plurals, past tenses, gerunds, agent nouns, comparatives and
        superlatives, and prefixed forms); chemical names varied by substituent, and short chains of root, infix, and suffix,
        made by substitution over a hand-written list of substituents and roots, with no tool that derives a name from a
        formula; medical and scientific prefixes; and Greek plurals. It gave each base word a domain label, and it used NLTK,
        lemminflect, and spaCy. The universe infers each generated row's method from its form, in
        <code>eol_synthetic_engine_inferred</code>, and keeps the recorded category in <code>eol_synthetic_category</code>.
      </p>
      <p>
        The universe keeps both of its lists, the valid and the invalid, and records what decided each valid row in
        <code>model_or_engine</code>. The charts below are computed from every one of its rows in version 1.0.
      </p>
    </section>

    <EolCharts client:load cubeUrl="/data/cube-v1.0.json" />

    <section class="eol-get" aria-labelledby="get">
      <h2 id="get">Getting it</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <a href="/compose/?d.eol=true">Compose it as a list</a>
          <p>Its rows of version 1.0, with the code that reproduces them, and any criterion of your own on top.</p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <a href="/dataset/profile/?d.eol=true">Profile it</a>
          <p>Every dimension of its rows, charted, with every filter of the profile.</p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <a href="https://huggingface.co/datasets/ryanjosephkamp/english-openlist" rel="noopener">Its own dataset</a>
          <p>The list as the English OpenList last published it, at <code>ryanjosephkamp/english-openlist</code>.</p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <a href="https://english-openlist.pages.dev/" rel="noopener">Its own site</a>
          <p>Search and filter the list as the English OpenList published it.</p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <a href="https://github.com/ryanjosephkamp/english-openlist" rel="noopener">Its own repository</a>
          <p>Its code, its rule, and its nightly run, at <code>ryanjosephkamp/english-openlist</code>.</p>
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p class="eol-note">
        The English OpenList keeps its own site, repository, and dataset. This page shows its dated subset in version 1.0.
      </p>
    </section>
  </div>
</Base>

<style>
  .eol {
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--space-7);
  }
  .eol-head {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: auto minmax(0, 1fr);
    gap: var(--space-5);
    align-items: start;
  }
  .eol-mark {
    margin-top: 0.4rem;
  }
  .eol-head > div {
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--space-3);
  }
  .eol-head h1 {
    font-size: var(--step-5);
  }
  .eol-sum {
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-size: var(--step-2);
    line-height: 1.35;
    color: var(--ink);
  }
  .eol-sum .is-eol {
    color: var(--eol);
  }
  .eol-facts > p:not(.eol-sum) {
    color: var(--ink-soft);
  }
  .eol-how h2,
  .eol-get h2 {
    font-size: var(--step-3);
    margin-top: 0;
  }
  :global(.eol-charts) {
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--space-7);
  }
  :global(.eol-pair) {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(min(100%, 26rem), 1fr));
    gap: var(--space-7);
  }
  .eol-get {
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--space-4);
    max-width: 52rem;
  }
  .eol-get ul {
    list-style: none;
    margin: 0;
    padding: 0;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--rule);
  }
  .eol-get li {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: minmax(11rem, 15rem) 1fr;
    gap: var(--space-2) var(--space-5);
    padding-block: var(--space-3);
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--rule);
    align-items: baseline;
  }
  .eol-get li a {
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-size: var(--step-1);
    font-weight: 600;
    text-decoration: none;
  }
  .eol-get li a:hover {
    text-decoration: underline;
  }
  .eol-get li p {
    color: var(--ink-soft);
  }
  @media (max-width: 36rem) {
    .eol-get li {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr;
    }
  }
  .eol-note {
    font-size: var(--step--1);
    color: var(--ink-soft);
  }
</style>
